NNAAMMEE
       inotify - monitoring filesystem events

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  _i_n_o_t_i_f_y API provides a mechanism for monitoring filesystem events.
       Inotify can be used to monitor individual files, or to monitor directo‐
       ries.  When a directory is monitored, inotify will  return  events  for
       the directory itself, and for files inside the directory.

       The following system calls are used with this API:

       •  iinnoottiiffyy__iinniitt(2)  creates  an inotify instance and returns a file de‐
          scriptor referring to the inotify instance.  The  more  recent  iinnoo‐‐
          ttiiffyy__iinniitt11(2) is like iinnoottiiffyy__iinniitt(2), but has a _f_l_a_g_s argument that
          provides access to some extra functionality.

       •  iinnoottiiffyy__aadddd__wwaattcchh(2) manipulates the "watch list" associated with an
          inotify  instance.   Each item ("watch") in the watch list specifies
          the pathname of a file or directory, along with some set  of  events
          that  the  kernel  should  monitor  for the file referred to by that
          pathname.  iinnoottiiffyy__aadddd__wwaattcchh(2) either creates a new watch item,  or
          modifies an existing watch.  Each watch has a unique "watch descrip‐
          tor",  an integer returned by iinnoottiiffyy__aadddd__wwaattcchh(2) when the watch is
          created.

       •  When events occur for monitored files and directories, those  events
          are made available to the application as structured data that can be
          read from the inotify file descriptor using rreeaadd(2) (see below).

       •  iinnoottiiffyy__rrmm__wwaattcchh(2) removes an item from an inotify watch list.

       •  When all file descriptors referring to an inotify instance have been
          closed (using cclloossee(2)), the underlying object and its resources are
          freed  for reuse by the kernel; all associated watches are automati‐
          cally freed.

       With careful programming, an application can use inotify to efficiently
       monitor and cache the state of a set of filesystem  objects.   However,
       robust applications should allow for the fact that bugs in the monitor‐
       ing  logic or races of the kind described below may leave the cache in‐
       consistent with the filesystem state.  It is probably wise to  do  some
       consistency  checking,  and  rebuild the cache when inconsistencies are
       detected.

